{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And YHWH came from Sinai.</b> He opened first with the praise of the Omnipresent and afterward opened with the needs of Israel — and the praise with which He opened contains mention of merit for Israel. And all this is the way of conciliation, that is to say: they are worthy that blessing should rest upon them (Sifrei)." "<b> Came from Sinai.</b> He went out to meet them when they came to station themselves at the foot of the mountain, like a bridegroom who goes out to receive the bride, as it says ״לִקְרַאת הָאֱלֹהִים״ (Exodus 19) — this teaches that He went out toward them." "<b>And He shone forth from Seir to them.</b> For He offered to the children of Esau that they receive the Torah, and they did not want to." "<b>He appeared from Mount Paran.</b> That He went there and offered to the children of Ishmael that they receive it, and they did not want to (Sifrei)." "<b>And He came.</b> To Israel." "<b>Myriads of holy ones.</b> And with Him a portion of the myriads of holy angels — not all of them and not the majority; and not the way of flesh and blood, who shows the entirety of his wealth's glory and splendor on the day of his wedding (ibid.)." "<b>A fiery law.</b> It was written from of old before Him in black fire upon white fire; He gave it to them on the tablets as the script of His right hand (cf. Tanhuma Bereshit 1). Another interpretation — <b>A fiery law</b> — as the Targum renders: that it was given to them from within the fire."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:49:41.726872646Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}